From 928caec4e1b0098922b81e7d65a4de02ff1ed55b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:21:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The clue hunter outfit, and it does three things OSRS's does
 not

Backlog item 7. Six pieces out of a modern OSRS cache, dropped by caskets, and with the full set on:
a chance the casket pays twice, and a chance a trail step is skipped.

OSRS'S CLUE HUNTER OUTFIT DOES NOTHING FOR CLUES. Leather-armour stats, and five of the six count
as warm clothing. No casket bonus, no step reduction, no drop-rate effect - the wiki page lists the
stats and stops. So every effect here was asked for by name and is recorded as a departure in
tools/cluehunterspec.json under what_osrs_actually_does, where a check reads it, so nobody later
reads these as canon. In OSRS the set is dug up at five fixed spots, three of which are places this
build does not have, so the source is Corey's as well.

THE SIGNS ARE THE REASON THERE IS A GENERATOR FOR SIX RECORDS. The item table stores a negative
bonus as an unsigned 32-bit int: the Helm of Raedwald's -6 magic attack comes back as 4294967290,
and writing that through would have put the largest magic attack bonus in the game on a leather
helmet. The spec holds the signed values, the generator writes them, and the battery checks two
different things about them - that each piece equals the spec, AND that no bonus anywhere is within
a thousand of an unconverted unsigned int, which is a property rather than a restatement.

WHAT THE SET DOES, and where each hook is:

  a doubled casket    10%, in the three ~trail_clue_<tier>_reward procs, right after each works out
                      how many times to roll. Twice the rolls on the same table is exactly two
                      caskets' worth - and the three tiers are NOT the same, so this is three
                      hooks and not one: easy rolls 2 + random(3), medium 3, hard 4.
  a skipped step      10%, in ~trail_clue_progress, which every tier goes through - so one hook
                      covers easy, medium and hard, and a fourth tier would be covered too. It
                      advances the counter a second time, and the completeness test in the caller
                      reads the higher number, so a skip can finish a trail outright.

Not a new idea in that file: ~trail_clue_<tier>_complete already finishes early when progress + 2
reaches maxsteps and a coin flip agrees. The outfit extends a mechanism that was already there.

They are two separate draws at two different moments, which is what "one roll each" meant, and a
check counts the draws rather than trusting the comment.

THE SET COMES OUT OF CASKETS at 1/750 a roll, per piece, and only for a piece the player has none
of in the inventory, the bank or worn. Two reasons for the ownership test: an untradeable outfit
piece arriving twice is pure clutter, and skipping the ones already found is what makes the rate
for a given piece exactly the number asked for until it is found.

Six independent waits at 1/750 is 750 x H(6) = 1,837 rolls for the set. Divided by each tier's own
average that is about 612 easy caskets, 459 medium or 367 hard - the "about 600" that was asked
for, after the first figure of 1/1500 was halved. The battery recomputes all four numbers from the
denominator and the roll counts it reads out of the three reward procs, so the spec cannot quietly
disagree with itself, and it would have caught a single casket figure quoted for all three tiers -
which is what the first draft had.

DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE RARE TABLES. Those reach 1/97 a roll through arithmetic that is derived and
commented in the reward scripts - 1/13 to a 108-reward table in OSRS, worked back to 1/97 here -
and six new entries would move every existing rare's rate and make that comment wrong. A separate
roll hits the asked-for number exactly and leaves them alone. A check holds it from the other end:
each of the six existing tables still has exactly as many cases as its own random(), and no clue
hunter piece appears in any of them.

NO DESTROY OP, though OSRS gives every piece one at inventory op 5. In this build opheld5 is Drop,
served globally by player/scripts/drop.rs2, so carrying OSRS's would take Drop off the item - the
same reason the Max cape folded its Spellbook op into Features. tradeable=no instead. And the warm
clothing is not carried either: there is no cold-damage mechanic here for it to do anything in, and
no param was invented to pretend otherwise.

THE FULL-SET TEST TAKES ITS SLOTS FROM THE OBJ CONFIG, not from a list in the battery. Looking a
piece up in the wrong slot is the mistake the Rogue outfit's check was written for; here the check
reads each piece's wearpos out of its own record and builds the line it expects, so moving a
wearpos moves the check with it.

32 mutations, each caught by its own check, none loose and none skipped - and this harness has the
ANCHOR UNIQUENESS RULE, which only two of the ten had. A find string matching twice is a mutation
that changes something other than what it names while still coming back red, which is worse than a
missing mutation because it looks like coverage. Every one of the 32 anchors was unique, checked by
the harness rather than by hand.

Two entries were wrong first and both are written down beside the fixed versions. One added a
redundant line instead of changing the shape of the thing being read, and survived - correctly. The
other flipped the helm's signs to prove a guard that can only fail if EVERY negative in the file
disappears, which no single edit does; it is named at the equality check instead, and the guard is
recorded as having no mutation of its own rather than being given a misleading one.

CI gets the two new tools. It also does not have gamemode, barrows, leprechaun or follower - four
batteries and four harnesses that exist and run only when somebody remembers - which is noted in
the workflow and the backlog rather than fixed here: barrows_mutate alone is 237 entries at about
five minutes and needs a sharding decision rather than a line.

